Docker Deploy

Installing docker is a prerequisite. The instructions differ depending on the
environment. Docker is comprised of two parts: the daemon server which runs on
Linux and accepts commands, and the client which is a Go program capable of
running on MacOS, all Unix variants and Windows.

Docker Installation

Follow the Docker install instructions (https://docs.docker.com/engine/installation/).

Available images

There are development and deploy images available.

Development

The development image is a bulky image containing a complete build toolchain.
It is well suited to hacking around and running the tests (including the
acceptance tests). To fetch this image, run ./builder.sh pull. The image can
be run conveniently via ./builder.sh.

Note that if you use the builder image, you should ensure that your
Docker installation grants 4GB or more of RAM to containers. On some
systems, the default configuration limits containers to 2GB memory
usage and this can be insufficient to build/link a CockroachDB
executable.

Deployment

The deploy image is a downsized image containing a minimal environment for
running Tardigrade. It is based on Debian Slim and contains only the main
cockroachoss binary. The Dockerfile lives at deploy/Dockerfile and is what
CI builds (.github/workflows/build.yml).

To build the image yourself, first build the OSS binary, then build the
image with the repository root as the build context. The binary is baked
into the minimal deployment container. The resulting image can be run via
docker run in the usual fashion. To be more specific, the steps are:

  $ make buildoss
  $ docker build -f deploy/Dockerfile -t tardigrade:19.2 .

The entrypoint wrapper is shared at build/deploy/cockroach.sh.

The list of valid/recognized targets is available in the script
build/builder/mkrelease.sh, for example amd64-linux-gnu and
amd64-darwin. Note that this script supports experimental targets
which may or may not work (and are not officially supported).

Upgrading / extending the Docker image

Process:

- edit build/builder/Dockerfile as desired
- run build/builder.sh init to test -- this will build the image locally. Beware this can take a lot of time. The result of init is a docker image version which you can subsequently stick into the version variable inside the builder.sh script for testing locally.
- Once you are happy with the result, run build/builder.sh push which pushes your image towards Docker hub, so that it becomes available to others. The result is again a version number, which you then must copy back into builder.sh. Then commit the change to both build/builder/Dockerfile and build/builder.sh and submit a PR.

Dependencies

Go dependencies are managed as modules (go.mod/go.sum) and resolved
from the module cache. There is no vendor/ directory. Run
`go mod download` once after cloning, then plain `go build` and
`go test` work from the repo root (the buildmod/testmod make
targets pass -mod=mod explicitly). Never run `go mod tidy`: it
chases test-only dependencies to latest and breaks the pinned
2019 graph. Pin a new import by hand with
`go list -m repo@sha` and append the require. See MODULES.md.

C++ dependencies (protobuf, rocksdb, snappy, libroach)
live in c-deps/ as submodules plus the tracked libroach tree,
and are built with the period builder image
(cockroachdb/builder:20191029-105705); see "C++ dependencies"
below and the buildoss CI job.

Updating the golang version

Please copy this checklist into the relevant commit message, with a link
back to this document:

* [ ] Adjust version in Docker image (source - /build/builder/Dockerfile#L199-L200).
* [ ] Rebuild the Docker image and bump the version in builder.sh accordingly (source - /build/builder.sh#L6).
* [ ] Bump the version in go-version-check.sh (source - /build/go-version-check.sh), unless bumping to a new patch release.

Updating Dependencies

Go dependencies: edit go.mod by hand (see "Dependencies" above).
Do not use dep; do not run `go mod tidy`.

C++ dependencies: c-deps/ holds git submodules (rocksdb,
snappy, protobuf) plus the tracked libroach
sources. Initialize
with `git submodule update --init` and build with the
builder image, e.g. `make libprotobuf
libsnappy librocksdb libroach` inside
cockroachdb/builder:20191029-105705. The Go link of the
full binary needs the resulting static libraries
(libroach.a, librocksdb.a, libprotobuf.a, libsnappy.a)
installed where the system linker finds them
(e.g. /usr/local/lib); see MODULES.md.
